** — 5-card sequence, medium rank, leaves 2s, jokers, and another straight option (9-K) intact.  \n- **Straight ♠9 ♥10 ♥J ♠Q ♠K** — higher rank but removes the K from potential pairs/triples.  \n- **Triple with attachments** — uses my powerful 2s early, which is risky since they control later rounds.  \n- **Four with pair/single** — uses bomb of 2s early, weakening my late-game dominance.  \n\nThe 8–Q straight is a balanced opener: strong enough to be hard to beat without high cards or sequences, yet conservative with my high bombs. Given opponents have 17 cards each, they likely can’t beat a 5-card straight easily unless they have a bomb or higher straight, which would cost them significant power.  \n\n**Reason**  \nLeading with a medium straight applies pressure, conserves bombs and rockets for counterattacks, and keeps flexibility in my hand (I still have another possible straight 9–K and plenty of high singles/pairs). It’s a controlling but not reckless opening.  \n\n**Answer**  \n{"cards": ["♣8", "♠9", "♥10", "♥J", "♠Q"]}'}
